# The fusions whose parts overlap, with the layer they both reach. One row per (fusion, layer
# reached through two of its own parts), carrying which two parts got there.
#
# Where the parts' claim to divide the layer is false
#   A fusion's parts claim to divide the composed layer between them without overlap, and this is
#   where that claim is false. Not a duplicate: a layer somebody referred to at one level while a
#   sibling part already carried it, so the parts overlap where nobody said so.
#   `asrt:Elimination` is the filer admitting an overlap and sizing it. This is an overlap that is
#   structural, visible in the parts, and unadmitted.
#
# The subject is the fusion, not the top of the composition
#   Every layer above the fusion also reaches the doubled layer twice, so a rule keyed on the top
#   would report one defect once per level above and name a composer who did nothing wrong. The
#   composer at fault is the one holding both parts, which is exactly the key here.
#
# A part reaches itself, on purpose
#   This composes `composition/reachable.sqlc`, where a layer counts as reaching itself, rather
#   than `composition/descent.sqlc`, where it does not. Naming a layer directly while a sibling
#   composes it is the common case, so a part must count as reaching itself or the commonest form
#   of this escapes: a holding naming a member's layer directly beside the group that composes it,
#   so that it reaches the member's layer once directly and again through the group. A population
#   written as the parts a fusion does not name cannot see it, because the layer reached directly
#   is a part the fusion does name.
#
# A key refuses it at one level, nothing at two
#   At one level the primary key on `pm.part` already refuses this, and nothing can at two, because
#   the second path runs through a document the first does not contain. That is the whole reason
#   this is a query.
#
# Not conditioned on a filed elimination
#   The key governs the second naming and the elimination governs the number, and folding an answer
#   about an amount into a structural population is how a rule stops being able to see the
#   structure.
#
# The pair comparison
#   It keeps each pair of parts once, in one order, and drops a part paired with itself, so a part
#   is never reported against itself.
#
# Probe
#   Nest a doubled layer three levels deep: keyed on the fusion this returns one row, where a key on
#   the top would return one per level above, against filings that did nothing wrong.
-- composition/parts.sqlc joined to itself on the fusion, against composition/descent.sqlc with
-- each layer reaching itself, for the layer two sibling parts both reach.
SELECT DISTINCT
       a.composition    AS filing,
       a.composed_layer AS layer,
       r1.filing        AS doubled_filing,
       r1.layer         AS doubled_layer,
       a.part_filing    AS via_filing,
       a.part_layer     AS via_layer,
       b.part_filing    AS also_via_filing,
       b.part_layer     AS also_via_layer
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) a
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) b ON  b.composition    = a.composition
    AND b.composed_layer = a.composed_layer
    AND (a.part_filing, a.part_layer) < (b.part_filing, b.part_layer)
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/reachable.sqlc)
) r1 ON r1.root_filing = a.part_filing AND r1.root_layer = a.part_layer
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/reachable.sqlc)
) r2 ON  r2.root_filing = b.part_filing AND r2.root_layer = b.part_layer
     AND r2.filing = r1.filing AND r2.layer = r1.layer
